diff --git a/docs/deployment.md b/docs/deployment.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..8e82910
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/deployment.md
@@ -0,0 +1,39 @@
+# Deploy Fizzy to one server
+
+This [Kamal](https://kamal-deploy.org/) configuration runs Fizzy, SQLite, and Solid Queue workers in one application container. Kamal Proxy handles HTTPS. You don't need a separate database, job server, or image registry account.
+
+## Before you start
+
+- Point your public hostname directly at a Linux server running Docker. Leave TCP ports 80 and 443 available to Kamal Proxy and reachable from the internet for Let's Encrypt.
+- Check that you can SSH to the server as `root`, or set `ssh.user` in `config/deploy.yml` to a user with Docker access.
+- On your development machine, have Docker with Buildx running. To use `bin/kamal`, install the repository's Ruby version and run `bundle install` in the OSS checkout (not SaaS mode). If you already have Kamal 2.11 installed, use `kamal` in place of `bin/kamal` below.
+
+## First deploy
+
+1. Edit [`config/deploy.yml`](../config/deploy.yml):
+   - Replace `fizzy.example.com` with your hostname, without `https://`. The proxy and links in emails use that same name.
+   - Set `SMTP_ADDRESS`, `SMTP_PORT`, and `MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS` for your mail provider. Use a sender the provider accepts. Fizzy uses TLS on port 465 and requires STARTTLS on other ports (normally 587).
+   - Set `builder.arch` to `arm64` if the server is ARM rather than x86-64.
+
+2. On your development machine, copy the secrets template:
+
+   ```sh
+   install -m 600 .kamal/secrets.example .kamal/secrets
+   ```
+
+   Fill in `SECRET_KEY_BASE` with the output of `openssl rand -hex 64`. Keep this value across deploys. Set `SMTP_USERNAME` and `SMTP_PASSWORD`, or leave both blank for a relay without authentication. These are dotenv values: use single quotes around passwords containing `$` or `#`.
+
+   For browser push, optionally [generate VAPID keys](development.md#web-push-notifications) and uncomment their entries in both `config/deploy.yml` and `.kamal/secrets`. Never commit `.kamal/secrets`.
+
+3. Commit your configuration changes (Kamal builds from Git), then run:
+
+   ```sh
+   bin/kamal config # Check the target host and storage mount
+   bin/kamal setup
+   ```
+
+   Visit `https://your-host` and enter your email address to create the first account. Fizzy will email you a sign-in code.
+
+## Subsequent deploys
+
+Commit your changes and run `bin/kamal deploy`. The `fizzy_storage` Docker volume mounted at `/rails/storage` holds the SQLite databases (including jobs, cache, and cable) and uploaded files. The entrypoint prepares the databases on each deploy; replacing the app container does not remove that volume. Keep the volume name and server unchanged, and back up the data and secrets separately.
